            <item>
                <title>Windows Server 2028 trades compatibility for out-of-the-box security</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/windows-server-2028-public-preview-bets-on-a-security-first-approach/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/windows-server-2028-public-preview-bets-on-a-security-first-approach/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Nick Indge]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:41:12 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[DISA STIG]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Microsoft Windows Server 2028 Public Preview]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Windows Server 2028]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/windows-server-2028-public-preview-bets-on-a-security-first-approach/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Hardened defaults align with DoD standards, but there may be growing pains.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Microsoft's Ignite announcements this week went beyond the <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/microsoft-calls-copilot-the-new-os-for-work-at-ignite-keynote/">Office-in-Copilot overhaul</a> with the reveal of the Windows Server 2028 Public Preview and its new security-by-default stance and what Microsoft calls <a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/windowsservernewsandbestpractices/announcing-hardened-server-core-for-windows-server-insiders/4574239">Hardened Server Core</a>. Windows Server 2028 will implement a default security framework that Microsoft says is aligned with hardened DoD benchmarks, such as the <a href="https://www.disa.mil/">Defense Information Systems Agency's</a> (DISA) <a href="https://www.cyber.mil/stigs/compilations/">Security Technical Implementation Guide</a> (STIG).</p>
<p>STIGs work by providing a standard security framework for system administrators to follow recommended guidance. In the past, Windows Server admins were left to start from a blank canvas and then implement hardening configurations for operations. Secure baselines were available but not the default. Microsoft says that concern is now managed: "The s<span data-contrast="auto">ecure configuration is the starting state, not a project you run after setup. That closes the window where a newly deployed server sits exposed while it waits to be hardened.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}">" </span></p>
<p>The new Windows Server 2028 hardened-by-default state will only be available for the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/server-core/what-is-server-core">Server Core</a> installs. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/install-options-server-core-desktop-experience">Windows Server Desktop Experience</a> installs do not include the changed stance.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/windows-server-2028-public-preview-bets-on-a-security-first-approach/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/11/windows-server-2028-public-preview-bets-on-a-security-first-approach/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
