                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/muse-privacy-invasions-are-freaking-out-meta-staffers-internal-chats-show/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Erratic Muse outputs sharing random bank, bitcoin balances aren’t real, Meta says.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>After some early adopters of Meta’s Muse AI agent complained that it was spying on private messages and behaving unpredictably earlier this fall, Reuters <a href="https://www.reuters.com/investigations/meta-bets-big-muse-ai-agent-early-safety-mishaps-surface-2026-11-18/">exposed</a> internal chats showing that Meta staff had raised the same alarms.</p>
<p>When testing the agent, staff warned that Muse appeared to be accessing encrypted private messages that users did not expect the bot to review, could be mixing up conversations between users, and even potentially sharing sensitive information users would never expect to be exposed.</p>
<p>Some unexpected Muse outputs responded to questions that staffers never asked.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/muse-privacy-invasions-are-freaking-out-meta-staffers-internal-chats-show/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/muse-privacy-invasions-are-freaking-out-meta-staffers-internal-chats-show/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
